new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f2e8da428bdef8e029815940b4161e048df5374e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/cpu-shadow.md
@@ -0,0 +1,212 @@
+# Ordinary guest RAM with Vulkan shadows
+
+`REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1` explicitly selects the Linux/Android CPU-shadow import
+path. The default is off. `0` also disables it; another value is an error. This
+is an experimental runtime backend, and selecting it does not establish that a
+device or guest is supported. Device creation fixes the runtime's CPU-shadow
+shader-address mode before any pipeline is compiled; importing a resource cannot
+change that mode afterward.
+
+The route is used by the real Vulkan platform's host-memory imports, including
+the EFI display's no-copy framebuffer. Guest CPU mappings continue to use their
+ordinary RAM files. The Metal-shaped runtime creates separate Vulkan storage,
+then copies bytes at its submission and completion boundaries. It does not
+export Vulkan memory as native guest RAM or run guest CPU atomics on that memory.
+
+## File identity and lifetime
+
+The existing `RvgDeviceConfig.memory_files` and guest-memory lease supply the
+backing; no QEMU callback or new configuration ABI is required. Each file must
+be an ordinary readable/writable memfd whose size cannot shrink (`F_SEAL_SHRINK`).
+`F_SEAL_WRITE` and `F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE` are refused because completion must be
+able to scatter GPU changes. QEMU must create RAM with sealing enabled and seal
+its size before registering it.
+
+The reims source resolves each import through the same table that records eager
+and deferred guest aliases. It returns owned descriptors, byte offsets and
+lengths in the resource's logical order. The runtime captures that answer once
+and retains it for the resource's lifetime. Unmapping or replacing the CPU alias
+does not retarget an already-created resource. Sub-page ranges retain their
+exact byte offsets and lengths. An unregistered byte or a CPU view without read
+permission is refused; it never falls through to a host-pointer or dma-buf
+import.
+
+Runtime canonicalization uses the retained file's device and inode plus its
+offset. Canonical allocations cover 1 MiB file chunks, while snapshots cover only
+the union of bytes in live views. This identifies repeated and overlapping
+physical bytes independently of descriptor numbers and CPU virtual addresses.
+The runtime preserves that identity when constructing its GPU views. A 4 KiB
+leaf table translates each
+actual shader word access, including wide/vector accesses and pointers loaded
+from guest buffers. The CPU host can have either 4 KiB or 16 KiB pages. Vulkan
+operations that require contiguous raw buffers use gathered views around their
+command boundaries. A logical layout or operation the runtime cannot represent
+is refused explicitly.
+
+Reims' read-only zero aliases need special treatment: a GPU write must not
+modify the process-wide zero source. As in the existing dma-buf import path,
+each import receives fresh ordinary zero-filled backing for those pieces.
+The runtime keeps that file after the one source resolution. Separate imports
+do not acquire a false shared-zero identity. Other read-only aliases retain the
+existing Vulkan platform's writes-kept marking: because a write would reach the
+writable file, commands that may write through such an object are refused when
+encoded, as on the existing Linux path.
+
+## Synchronization contract
+
+The runtime serializes shadow preparation with earlier GPU completion. It
+snapshots the CPU-authoritative bytes, uploads them, and executes the batch.
+After that batch's GPU completion it compares the Vulkan bytes with the
+snapshot and scatters only changed byte runs to ordinary RAM. Scatter must
+finish before shared events, completion handlers or subsequent host steps expose
+the result. A preparation or scatter failure prevents a successful handoff.
+
+This preserves CPU writes to bytes the GPU did not change. It does not make
+unsynchronized CPU and GPU writes to the same bytes coherent; callers must use
+the guest API's resource and event ordering. The backend never acquires QEMU's
+big lock or pauses vCPUs from a GPU callback. QEMU can be waiting for a FIFO
+operation while holding that lock.
+
+Raw external semaphore signaling is refused in this mode: a Vulkan semaphore
+alone could become visible before the ordinary-RAM scatter. The QEMU Android
+display path uses offscreen frame targets and completed readback; the optional
+reims desktop window/swapchain path uses external binary signals and is outside
+this mode's current supported scope. The backend also does not advertise
+resource detach or heap/buffer address-range construction until their lifecycle
+is qualified. It does not infer those capabilities from the driver's unrelated
+host-pointer import extension.
+
+## Observing actual work
+
+The additive query reads the runtime used by an existing guest device:
+
+```c
+RvgCpuShadowCounts counts = {0};
+RvgStatus status = rvg_device_cpu_shadow_counts(device, &counts, sizeof counts);
+```
+
+It writes `struct_size`, a zero `reserved` field, and six cumulative counters:
+
+| Counter | Evidence |
+| --- | --- |
+| `chunks_created` | Canonical Vulkan backing chunks were created |
+| `views_created` | Logical resources acquired those backings |
+| `epochs_prepared` | CPU snapshots were uploaded for GPU batches |
+| `epochs_finished` | Completed batches finished delta scatter |
+| `bytes_uploaded` | Ordinary CPU bytes were copied into Vulkan storage |
+| `bytes_scattered` | GPU-changed bytes were written back to ordinary RAM |
+
+The counts belong to the runtime device, which can be shared by several guest
+devices. They are diagnostic snapshots and do not wait for GPU work. Observe
+the frame or event's normal completion before checking a submission's effects.
+An enabled environment variable alone changes no counter.
+
+
+The optional `cpu-shadow-probe` feature adds a bounded installed-runtime check:
+
+```c
+RvgCpuShadowProbeResult result = {0};
+RvgStatus status = rvg_cpu_shadow_probe_v1(10000, &result, sizeof result);
+```
+
+The entry point always exists; a build without the feature returns unsupported.
+The feature forwards from `reims-vgpu-c` through `reims-vgpu` to the Vulkan
+platform. On Linux and Android its build helper assembles the repository's own
+LLVM fixture with `RVG_CPU_SHADOW_LLVM_AS`, or `llvm-as` if the variable is absent.
+Ordinary builds without the feature acquire no assembler requirement.
+
+Run this check in a fresh disposable process, with CPU-shadow mode selected
+before any platform use and no concurrent guest or GPU work. The caller must
+also impose a process deadline: the 100–30000 ms argument bounds GPU completion
+waits, but cannot cancel a driver call or shader compilation. Invalid arguments,
+unsupported builds and failed checks leave the output unchanged. Success writes
+revision 1 and the complete check mask `0x7f`, after actual no-copy fragmented
+imports, same-invocation alias atomics, a disjoint CPU edit, event publication
+after scatter, exact ordinary-file comparisons and fragmented fill/blit
+readbacks have completed. It reports actual counters and Vulkan identity,
+including the device type and device/driver UUIDs from the same selected device.
+A software device's pass does not establish a phone GPU's behavior. The probe
+does not attest source provenance or replace guest boot qualification.
+
+## Validation and remaining scope
+
+The provider's focused tests cover captured descriptor lifetime, reordered and
+repeated pages, read-only deferred pages, exact sub-page ranges, private zeros,
+unregistered ranges and invalid policy values. They validate source resolution,
+not GPU execution. The C query's boundary tests also do not establish driver
+support.
+
+`examples/cpu_shadow_alias.rs` adds a GPU check through reims' actual no-copy
+import methods. It registers two different CPU mappings and duplicated
+descriptors of one memfd. An authored kernel performs two atomic adds through
+overlapping buffer views in one invocation; the returned old values must prove
+that both views reach the same GPU word. The check also changes a disjoint CPU
+word after upload, checks that delta scatter preserves it, and reads the GPU
+result as soon as a shared event is visible. All CPU accesses to the source use
+the ordinary memfd. Its AIR input is assembled from the small authored LLVM
+fixture, without an Apple binary:
+
+```sh
+llvm-as crates/reims-vgpu-vulkan/examples/cpu_shadow_alias/kernel.ll -o /path/to/kernel.air
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 cargo run -p reims-vgpu-vulkan --example cpu_shadow_alias -- /path/to/kernel.air
+```
+
+Its `--fragmented` case maps four logical 16 KiB regions to physical file regions
+`[0, 2, 1, 2]`, then imports a separate alias of region 2. Atomic operations in
+one invocation must observe both kinds of alias. Vector and 64-bit loads/stores
+cross noncontiguous leaf boundaries. A guest GPU pointer loaded from ordinary
+RAM also crosses that boundary through the runtime's dynamic address table.
+Every source-file byte is compared afterward, including the disjoint CPU edit.
+
+The `--chunk-boundary` case places those regions across a 1 MiB canonical
+allocation boundary, compares the entire larger file, and requires two chunks.
+The kernel also checks null, one-past-owned-root, known unmapped dynamic and
+large static offsets; out-of-range stores must leave the full source unchanged.
+These assertions are retained when a compiler defect is found.
+
+`examples/cpu_shadow_fixed.rs` covers segmented blit, byte fill, overlapping
+copies through distinct aliases, and indexed draws after CPU/GPU updates. Its
+owned-index control distinguishes basic authored-shader/geometry errors from
+paged fixed-function gather errors. CPU texture reads and replacements continue
+while the fixture's original mapping is `PROT_NONE`, checking that deferred work
+uses retained file descriptors.
+
+`examples/cpu_shadow_rebind.rs` drives public reims buffer detach/replacement
+through direct, fragmented and direct backings while retaining an existing
+texture. `examples/cpu_shadow_heap.rs` covers shared heap placement, a texture
+crossing physical regions, CPU texture replacement, GPU refresh and heap backing
+replacement. Both compare whole old and new files and require stable resource
+identities and GPU addresses. These lower-level checks do not advertise guest
+capabilities by themselves. `examples/cpu_shadow_lifetime.rs` checks an encoded
+use's refusal, replay of work committed behind an event and old-backing retention
+while submitted work is still in flight, including source and release counts.
+
+`examples/cpu_shadow_tile.rs` uses the
+existing authored float framebuffer-fetch shaders with the actual tile-image
+compiled metadata. It reads pixels back through an ordinary-memfd shadow and
+checks padding. A driver without coherent tile color reads exits explicitly
+unsupported; a host CPU ICD cannot qualify the Pixel's tile path.
+
+One executable includes these cases to avoid transferring duplicate copies of
+the compiler/runtime to Android:
+
+```sh
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification alias cpu-shadow-alias.air
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification fragmented cpu-shadow-alias.air
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification chunk-boundary cpu-shadow-alias.air
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification fixed cpu-shadow-fixed.air
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification rebind
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification lifetime
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification heap
+REIMS_VGPU_CPU_SHADOW=1 ./cpu_shadow_qualification tile cpu-shadow-tile-vertex.air cpu-shadow-tile-fragment.air
+```
+
+The full application remains gated on runtime integration checks and a guest
+boot. The guest-facing detach/address-range capability answers remain withheld
+in this mode; those flags also affect shared-heap and argument-buffer routes in
+core, so enabling them needs explicit guest-path qualification. Backing
+replacement, heap and texture operations must either preserve the same canonical
+bytes or report a typed refusal. The shader interpreter suite,
+actual GPU execution, physical PowerVR execution, and a native-EL0 guest boot are
+separate evidence. A passing contiguous EFI-framebuffer test alone does not
+qualify those operations or an iOS desktop.
